  <title>4.2.4: Understanding Function Notation Using Application Problems</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00859</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.2.4: Understanding Function Notation Using Application Problems</md:title>
    <md:uuid>16d0f283-eeba-4fd7-8bc7-b2079acf9da5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Function \(Q\) gives a puppy’s weight, in pounds, as a function of its age, in months.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What does each expression or equation represent in this situation?
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item>\(Q(18)\)</item>
      <item>\(Q(30)=27.5\)</item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>  A. the weight of the puppy when it was 18 months old 
  B. When the puppy was 30 months old, it weighed 27.5 pounds. </para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use function notation to represent each statement.
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item>When the puppy turned 12 months old, it weighed 19.6 pounds.</item>
      <item>When the puppy was \(m\) months old, it weighed \(w\) pounds.</item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>


<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>  A. \(Q(12)=19.6\)
  B. \(Q(m)=w\)</para>

  
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>Students will have more opportunities to understand the mathematical ideas in this cool-down, so there is no need to slow down or add additional work to the next lessons. Instead, use the results of this cool-down to provide guidance for what to look for and emphasize over the next several lessons to support students in advancing their current understanding.</para></section>
</content>
